<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 Unit 5 lesson 18 original 2021 source-aligned optional design">
<h2>Design With Fractions — OPTIONAL project</h2>
<p>Paper and ruler/straightedge. One complete side is the unit at EACH new iteration. Teacher checks drawn side marks, endpoint choice, connected points, discussions and observations; this is NOT an auto-graded numeric quiz.</p>
<h3>Warm-up: four different-scale zero-to-one number lines</h3><p>Each line goes from 0 to1, but the drawing lengths differ. Three originally show midpoints; the fourth is blank. Invite noticing/wondering and ask where the blank line's 1/2 and then fourths would fall, relative to THAT whole.</p>
<h3>18.1 — 1/2 of each side: all three original steps</h3><ol><li>Mark each side midpoint; connect adjacent marked points and identify the new shape.</li><li>Repeat on each side of the new shape and identify the result.</li><li>Repeat at least TWO MORE times and discuss the emerging design.</li></ol>
<svg viewBox="0 0 360 360" width="320" height="320" role="img" aria-label="Independently drawn nested squares: midpoint square, rotated diamond, smaller aligned square and repeated two more times">
<polygon points="40,40 320,40 320,320 40,320" fill="none" stroke="#3b5b79" stroke-width="2"/>
<polygon points="180,40 320,180 180,320 40,180" fill="none" stroke="#377d90" stroke-width="2"/>
<polygon points="110,110 250,110 250,250 110,250" fill="none" stroke="#755da2" stroke-width="2"/>
<polygon points="180,110 250,180 180,250 110,180" fill="none" stroke="#377d90" stroke-width="2"/>
<polygon points="145,145 215,145 215,215 145,215" fill="none" stroke="#755da2" stroke-width="2"/>
</svg>
<p>The original midway location is invariant to choosing either endpoint. Precise repetitions yield squares alternating orientation.</p>
<h3>18.2 — 1/4 of each side: all three original steps</h3><ol><li>Mark a quarter along each side of ANOTHER square from the chosen endpoint; connect adjacent marks.</li><li>Repeat the quarter-mark process on each NEW side, connect and identify the shape.</li><li>Attempt TWO MORE repetitions and observe; explain if a thin shape becomes impractical.</li></ol>
<svg viewBox="0 0 360 360" width="320" height="320" role="img" aria-label="Independent example of a quarter-mark design: a square and an uneven quadrilateral based on differently chosen starting endpoints">
<polygon points="40,40 320,40 320,320 40,320" fill="none" stroke="#3b5b79" stroke-width="2"/>
<polygon points="110,40 320,110 110,320 40,250" fill="none" stroke="#9a6842" stroke-width="2"/>
<circle cx="110" cy="40" r="4" fill="#9a6842"/><circle cx="320" cy="110" r="4" fill="#9a6842"/><circle cx="110" cy="320" r="4" fill="#9a6842"/><circle cx="40" cy="250" r="4" fill="#9a6842"/>
</svg>
<p>Different starting endpoints can yield squares or other quadrilaterals; the source does not require every student to produce one fixed quarter design.</p>
<h3>Teacher evidence</h3><p>Record use of straightedge or folding, four equal side-fraction distances, valid point connections, three original steps per activity, repetitions and explanation. Check similarities/differences with marking fractional positions on number lines. Creative extensions are OPTIONAL.</p>
<p>Original <a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-5/lesson-18/lesson.html">2021 teacher lesson</a>. This display uses original code and not publisher art.</p></section>